 # Sparse and Efficient Representations
 
-Also called: Sparse NeRFs, Distributed NeRFs
 
 ## Overview
-Sparse and Efficient Representations address the computational and memory bottlenecks of dense Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) by decomposing the scene representation into localized, sparse, or distributed components. Unlike monolithic NeRF implementations that evaluate a single deep network for every sample along a ray, these methods partition the 3D space or the model parameters to enable parallel training, reduce memory consumption, and accelerate rendering [S1]. They set themselves apart from dense MLP-based representations by explicitly leveraging sparsity (via voxels or spatial cells) or hardware distribution to scale to large-scale datasets [S1].
+Sparse and Efficient Representations address the prohibitive memory and computational costs of dense neural radiance fields (NeRFs) by decomposing the scene representation into localized, sparse, or hierarchical structures. Unlike monolithic MLPs that evaluate a single deep network for every sample, these methods partition the 3D space or the network parameters to enable parallel training, reduce memory overhead, and accelerate rendering [S1]. They set themselves apart from dense alternatives by explicitly managing spatial locality or parameter distribution, allowing for the reconstruction of large-scale scenes or real-time inference that would otherwise be infeasible [S1].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The core mechanism remains the parametric function $F_\Theta$ that maps a 3D position $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and a viewing direction $\mathbf{d} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ to a volume density $\sigma \in \mathbb{R}$ and an RGB color $\mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^3$. This function is typically implemented as one or more neural networks.
+The core mechanism remains the differentiable approximation of the continuous radiance field using a neural network or collection of networks, allowing gradient-based optimization [Parent, S1, S3].
 
