 ## Background
-The concept of sparse and efficient representations in NeRFs emerged to address the scalability limitations of the original NeRF architecture, which struggled with large scenes and high computational costs. Mega-NeRF introduced spatial partitioning to handle large-scale environments [S1]. Sparse Radiance Fields (SRF) explored voxel-based sparsity for efficient storage and rendering [S1]. KiloNeRF demonstrated the viability of distilling large NeRFs into grids of small networks for speed [S1][S2, unofficial]. ProNeRF focused on optimizing the sampling process itself to reduce the number of required network queries [S1]. NeRF-XL extended these concepts to multi-GPU distributed training, allowing for the scaling of hash-based NeRFs like Instant-NGP across multiple devices [S1].
+The concept of sparse and efficient representations in neural radiance fields emerged as a response to the scalability limits of the original NeRF architecture, which required dense sampling and deep MLP evaluation for every ray. Early works like KiloNeRF and Mega-NeRF introduced spatial decomposition to distribute the computational load [S1]. The Sparse Radiance Field (SRF) approach further explored explicit voxel-based sparsity to reduce memory footprint [S1]. More recently, methods like ProNeRF have focused on optimizing the sampling strategy itself to reduce the number of required network queries [S1]. The NeRF-XL approach specifically addresses multi-GPU scaling by partitioning parameters rather than just data, ensuring consistency across distributed training [S1].
 
